Note 1 — certifi (MPL-2.0)
--------------------------
The Mozilla Public License 2.0 is file-level copyleft: its obligations attach to
the MPL-licensed files themselves and not to software that merely depends on
them. De-Scramble does not modify, vendor, or redistribute modified copies of
certifi, so its own Apache-2.0 licensing is unaffected. Recorded here so the
decision is visible rather than implicit.


Note 2 — igraph (GPL) — UNRESOLVED
----------------------------------
igraph is published under the GNU General Public License and is a hard,
non-optional dependency of splink, which is the record-linkage engine this
project is built on. It is therefore installed on every machine that runs
`pip install descramble`, even though De-Scramble does not use it:

  - De-Scramble contains no igraph code and imports it nowhere.
  - splink does not import igraph when it is imported; the dependency is
    exercised only by graph-metrics features this project does not call.
  - The pipeline was verified to run correctly, producing identical clustering,
    with igraph uninstalled.

De-Scramble's own source remains Apache-2.0 and is unaffected. What is affected
is the environment a user ends up with: installing this project also installs
GPL-licensed software they may not have expected. Anyone redistributing a built
environment containing igraph must comply with the GPL for that component.

This is stated plainly because a licensing surprise discovered later is far
worse than one disclosed up front. It is tracked as an open issue and is not
considered settled.
